<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Are 3D ear scans as accurate as traditional impressions?</h3>
<p>In skilled hands, both methods can produce excellent results. Digital scanning reduces variability and speeds up remakes because the file is stored and editable. Many clinics still use traditional impressions for very deep fits or complex ear canals, then scan the impression for digital processing. The key is clinician technique and verification after the mold is made.</p>

<h3>Will custom earmolds stop feedback and make speech clearer?</h3>
<p>A good seal often reduces feedback and can allow your audiologist to deliver the right amplification—especially in the bass—without squeal. That can improve comfort and clarity. However, clarity also depends on your hearing aid settings, microphones, and real‑ear verification. Ask for a REM check after you get your new molds so your programming matches the new acoustics.</p>

<h3>Can custom earplugs replace earmuffs at work?</h3>
<p>Sometimes. It depends on the noise level and type of exposure. Properly fitted custom plugs can provide strong protection, and musician filters can make communication easier. In very loud environments, or with impulse noise, dual protection (plugs plus muffs) may be recommended. Your safety officer or audiologist can review your noise exposure and choose the right protection—and even fit‑test your plugs.</p>

<h3>How long do custom earmolds or earplugs last?</h3>
<p>Two to five years is typical for adults, but it varies with material, earwax, and changes in your ear over time. If fit or comfort changes—or your own voice suddenly feels different—book a quick check. Many labs offer a 30–90 day remake window, so use that period to dial in your fit.</p>


